This is a source-available licence, not an open source licence. It restricts
commercial use, which the Open Source Definition does not allow. It is written
plainly on purpose. If anything below is unclear, ask before assuming.


1. WHAT YOU MAY DO FREELY

You may run this software, read it, change it, and share your changes, for any
purpose that is not commercial. That includes learning from it, using it for
personal projects, using it in research and teaching, and making videos you do
not earn from.

Non-commercial use has no obligations attached. You do not owe attribution, you
do not need a grant, and you may remove the mark described in section 4.


2. WHAT COMMERCIAL USE MEANS

Commercial use is using this software, or anything it produces, to make money
or to try to. Without limiting that, it includes:

  (a) publishing output on a channel or account that is monetised, or that you
      are working towards monetising;
  (b) output used in advertising, sponsorship, or promotion of any business;
  (c) client or freelance work;
  (d) use inside an organisation that operates for profit;
  (e) selling, licensing, or hosting this software or a derivative of it as a
      product or service.


3. HOW TO GET COMMERCIAL RIGHTS

Contribute something, and have it accepted.

Any accepted contribution earns you a perpetual, non-transferable commercial
grant covering your own use of this software and its output. It does not have
to be code. Fixing a typo in the documentation counts. Reporting a bug that
turns out to be real counts. Improving a wrong explanation counts. Helping
somebody in an issue thread counts. There is no minimum size, and the grant is
not revoked if you never contribute again.

The maintainer may also issue a grant for any other reason, or by agreement.

CONTRIBUTING.md explains how to make a first contribution, written for people
who have never contributed to anything before.

A grant is issued as a signed file. Installing it turns off the attribution
mark described in section 4:

    python studio.py licence grant.json


4. THE ATTRIBUTION MARK

Video this software produces carries a small mark in the corner naming Santa
Studio, unless a commercial grant is installed.

For non-commercial use, you may remove it. Change the code, disable it, cut it
out of the file afterwards - all of that is fine, because you are not selling
anything and the credit was only ever a courtesy.

For commercial use, you may not. Removing, hiding, cropping out or disabling
the mark - or distributing a version that does - is only permitted with a
commercial grant. Using this software commercially without a grant is already
outside section 1; doing it with the mark stripped is that plus this.

The software will not stop you. Nothing running on your own machine could, and
this project does not pretend otherwise. What makes it a breach is this
section, not the code.


5. THE NAME AND THE LOGO

"Santa Studio", the Santa Studio logo, and the wordmark are trademarks of the
copyright holder. This licence gives you no rights to them.

You may say your work was made with Santa Studio. You may not name your own
product Santa Studio, use the logo as your own, or present a fork in a way that
suggests it is the official project or endorsed by it. This applies whether or
not you hold a commercial grant.


6. CONTRIBUTIONS

By submitting a contribution you licence it to the project under this same
licence, and you confirm it is yours to give.


7. IF YOU BREAK THIS LICENCE

Your rights under it end. They come back if you fix the breach within 30 days
of being told about it, and this is the first time.


